SCORE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=4 | implementability=4 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | IN_SCOPE | One pathway: top-8 training successor neighbourhood -> per-item Frechet anchor a_i -> r_1 = log_{a_i}(p_i) -> p-hat_l = exp_{p-hat_{l-1}}(q_l), so removing the anchor or the curvature changes the code tuple; the behaviour-off (a_i = 0), geometry-off (Euclidean chart) and both-on configurations are named in HYPOTHESIS, observables (i)-(iv) and a Stage-3 target are stated, and cost is bounded. Only weakness: the Euclidean counterpart is named but not written out, and no clamp/guard is given for the anchor magnitude.
EVIDENCE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | E1 E2 E3 E5 E8 E11 E12 E13
OBJECTION: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | Behaviour enters only through the base point a_i, so the mechanism is "subtract a behaviour-derived offset, then run the residual chain in the resulting tangent frame"; a fixed-curvature Mobius translation is a deterministic reparameterisation, and the proposal's own risk (1) says encoder collapse to the origin gives a_i ~ 0 for all items, which turns the run into the behaviour-off arm with no diagnostic able to tell "coupling works" from "the anchor was never used", because diagnostics (i)-(iv) read the emitted codes, never ||a_i||.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | If ||p_i|| <= 0.01 s for all i then log_{a_i}(p_i) = (p_i - a_i) + O(s^2), the geodesic chain is a first-order translation of the flat chain, and the emitted tuple equals the Euclidean RQ-VAE tuple up to a codebook reindexing - the both-on run is the geometry-off run relabelled.
RATIONALE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | 3: transitions are measured (E5 heterogeneity; E11 0.0907 vs 0.067 permuted) but "equal geodesic displacement beats equal position" is untested. 3: the curved primitive is explicit and per-item, but the matched Euclidean chart is only implied and the translation/rescaling reduction is not ruled out. 2: same family as the measured successor_geodesic_coarse (0.05500) and tangent_successor_residual (0.05500) in E6 - a routine variation, not a rename. 4: level-1 code agreement and prefix length are named and the assignment that changes (level-1 base) is identified. 4: three named configurations plus four diagnostics, but no explicit falsifier. 4: differentiable straight-through argmin, radius clamped to 0.5 s, EMA anchors, ~1.05x cost; remaining risk is the moving quantization target. 2: a negative would largely repeat the measured geodesic-residual negative. UNKNOWN: whether ||a_i|| is non-degenerate; bounded check is the histogram of ||a_i||/s at epoch 3000.

SCORE: BRANCHCURV-SID | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: BRANCHCURV-SID | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour (per-code successor branching entropy H_k via EMA over training-only transition rows) enters the nearest-code distance d_{kappa_k}(u_i,e_k) and hence occupancy, codebook gradient and SID; curvature-off (kappa_k = k0) and Euclidean both change the assignment. The three switch names are only implicit ("no ablation arm trained") and no matched Euclidean control is written out.
EVIDENCE: BRANCHCURV-SID | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E8 E10 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: BRANCHCURV-SID | Scale/domain mismatch inside its own spec: u_i = exp_0^{k0}(z_i) is a point of the k0 ball of radius 1/sqrt(|k0|), but it is scored by d_{kappa_k} with kappa_k up to 4*k0, whose ball radius is half that; for items beyond half the k0 radius the factor (1 - kappa_k||u_i||^2) is negative and the arcosh argument falls below 1. It is not implementable as written without an added re-mapping of u_i into each code's ball, and that re-mapping changes the mechanism.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: BRANCHCURV-SID | ||u_i||^2 = 0.9/|k0| and kappa_k = 4|k0| gives 1 - 3.6 = -2.6, so 1 + 2*kappa_k*||u_i-e_k||^2/((-2.6)(...)) < 1 and d^2 is NaN/complex; the softmax over that code is undefined.
RATIONALE: BRANCHCURV-SID | 4: the behavioural problem "predictability is heterogeneous" is measured with support and coverage (E5 bins, item counts, top1 0.4375 vs 0.0543, Spearman -0.973); it stops at 4 because the step from item entropy to code entropy is unmeasured. 3: curvature genuinely enters the distance, but the per-code curvature acts largely as a per-code distance rescaling a Euclidean metric weight plausibly reproduces, and no matched control is argued. 3: not in E6's ledger, distinct, though the behaviour->metric->quantizer family is crowded. 4: names the assignment change (occupancy/entropy gap), observables and direction. 3: three controls exist in principle plus a post-500 freeze, but they are not enumerated and no failure criterion is stated. 3: implementable once the k0/kappa_k mapping hole is closed; tau is derived, clamp documented. 3: a different lever from the measured Euclidean arms, but the likely degenerate field is half-admitted. UNKNOWN: how heterogeneous per-code entropies are; bounded check is the epoch-0 histogram of H_k at level 1.

SCORE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=1 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=1
SCOPE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour (mean successor displacement t_i in the tangent at the origin) and curvature (exp/log at k0) meet in one primitive s_i = exp_0^k(log_0^k(u_i) + eta*t_i) that drives the level-1 base and every later tangent frame; removing t_i or the maps changes the code chain. The geometry-off arm is not written out and no radius clamp is specified despite admitted boundary fragility.
EVIDENCE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E8 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: SUCC-TAN-RQ | The curvature is fixed (k0 = -1/median||z||^2, no trainable rho), so log_0^k and exp_0^k compose into one fixed radial diffeomorphism R and s_i = R^{-1}(R(u_i) + eta*t_i). Behaviour is carried entirely by the Euclidean tangent mean t_i and the curvature cancels around the quantizer - exactly the "curved operation a matched Euclidean transform reproduces" case.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | With fixed k, log_0(u) = 2 artanh(sqrt(k)||u||) u/(sqrt(k)||u||) and exp_0 is its inverse, so exp_0(log_0(u)+t) = R^{-1}(R(u)+t) with R independent of behaviour; a Euclidean quantizer fed R(u) with the same codebook mapped by R produces bit-identical code indices.
RATIONALE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | 3: E5 shows direction is informative only for low-entropy items (top1 0.4375 in bin 1) and the mean displacement at support 44.3 cancels, so the signal is real but thin with no per-item guard. 2: fixed curvature factors out as shown. 1: E6 already contains tangent_successor_residual (recall@10 0.05500, prefix alive 0.643/0.180/0.090/0.073), which is this mechanism; a rename with a different shift length. 4: assignment change and observables named. 3: three controls implied, no failure criterion beyond "above 0.065". 3: eta derived from median code spacing, but no clamp. 1: near-repeat of a measured mechanism at 0.055, so a negative adds almost nothing.

SCORE: GEOPFX-SID | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: GEOPFX-SID | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour (sampled transitions with counts, weighted by w_ij and gated by LCP) and curvature (geodesic segment and point-to-segment distance) meet in one term on the shared prefix anchors of the same codebook that emits the SIDs; behaviour-off (pairs dropped / uniform w) and geometry-off (Euclidean chord distance) both change it. Missing: the three arms and the Euclidean control are not written out, and the weight 1 is a documented default rather than a derived quantity.
EVIDENCE: GEOPFX-SID | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E8 E11 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: GEOPFX-SID | The term is inert exactly where it is needed: LCP = 0 pairs are skipped and E11 measures same_first_prefix_rate at 0.0907, so at epoch 0 roughly 91% of sampled transition pairs contribute nothing; the surviving 9% are already prefix-sharing (easy) pairs, and their gradient moves the shared anchor toward the u_i-u_j segment without changing which codes are selected, so betweenness can improve while LCP does not.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GEOPFX-SID | At 2048 sampled pairs and the 0.0907 prefix-agreement rate (E11) about 186 pairs are active; if the active anchors already lie on the segment (delta = 0) then L_geo and all its gradients are exactly 0 while the prefix hit-rate curve is unchanged - indistinguishable from both-off.
RATIONALE: GEOPFX-SID | 3: transitions are measured (E10 edges, E5 heterogeneity) and E11 gives a measured prefix-behaviour link, but 0.0907 vs 0.067 is a weak effect and the betweenness premise is unmeasured. 3: point-to-geodesic distance on the ball is genuinely curved (arc geodesics) and the term is scale-normalised, but no argument is given for why a Euclidean chord cannot reproduce the ranking. 3: prefix-anchor betweenness is not in E6's ledger; distinct, though successor_geodesic_coarse is a nearby behaviour geodesic. 3: chain is plausible but has the LCP-inertness gap. 3: three controls and a normalisation, no explicit failure criterion. 3: 12-step golden-section min is differentiable only as an unrolled argmin and LCP is discrete; eps and unit weight specified, cost under +10%. 3: different mechanism with a clear lesson (betweenness does not buy prefix survival) if it fails.

SCORE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=2 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour (leaf mass B_g of a Leiden community tree on the symmetrised training transition graph) sets the radius rho_lg of each atom via artanh, acting in codebook parameterisation and the per-step geodesic projection, i.e. inside quantization; a matched Euclidean control is declared (E15). Missing: the projection half-width delta is an undetermined constant entering the mechanism, so the no-tuning policy is not satisfied.
EVIDENCE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E8 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: GEO-SHELL-RQ | The advertised observable is a tautology: the hypothesis is that "broad communities occupy large-radius shells", but rho_lg is defined as an increasing function of B_g, so that histogram is true by construction; the only informative claim (radius -> prefix predictability) has no mechanism, because radius is one scalar and two communities with equal leaf mass and disjoint items share a shell with no angular separation.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | B_g = B_g' gives rho_lg = rho_lg' exactly; if their Frechet directions differ by pi the shell radius carries zero bits distinguishing them, so the radius diagnostic cannot separate a working mechanism from a relabelled baseline.
RATIONALE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | 3: the branching statistic is measured (E5; E8 shows 0.3106 at K=16 vs 0.0822 at K=256), but leaf-mass -> code radius is an unmeasured leap. 2: artanh(B_g) is a monotone radial coordinate and a Euclidean codebook at radius r = artanh(...) is a close surrogate, with no argument against it despite E15. 2: hyperbolic radius placement is a routine variation of standard hyperbolic embedding. 2: the chain from "big-radius shell" to "token predictable" has a gap (radius is not a prefix). 3: three configurations and a written matched control, though delta is free. 3: tree construction and Karcher means specified, projection bounds the atom, but delta is undefined and hard projection can freeze atoms. 3: a different failure mode (curvature-as-capacity) with an informative negative.

SCORE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=4 | implementability=4 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour (successor set S_i and its concentration c_i) fixes the interpolation fraction eta_i of a geodesic displacement of the reconstruction target, so behaviour changes the gradient shaping encoder and codebooks and hence the SID; eta_i = 0 is an exact behaviour-off recovering the baseline and Euclidean linear interpolation is the geometry-off control (E15). No arm is trained.
EVIDENCE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E8 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | Shifting the target toward the successor barycentre destroys the identity information the SID must carry: two items with the same successor set and concentration get the same target and therefore the same nearest codes, so distinct SIDs fall, and at Stage 3 one item is emitted per SID - the mechanism caps recall for exactly the items it merges. No diagnostic separates "role encoding helped" from "collisions ate the gain".
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | S_i = S_i' = {j} with c_i = c_i' = 1 gives eta = 1/2 and b_i = exp_{p_i}(0.5 log_{p_i}(p_j)); if ||p_i - p_i'|| is small against code spacing both quantise to the same (k1,k2,k3) and the distinct-SID count drops with no compensating prefix gain.
RATIONALE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | 4: c_i is exactly the measured top-1 mass of E5, auditable per bin (0.4375 -> 0.0543 with counts), and concentration is the right scalar for eta_i. 3: exp_p(t log_p q) is gyro-interpolation, genuinely different from the chord, but the difference is second order for t <= 1/2 and the value over the E15 control is not argued. 3: changing the reconstruction target (not a loss, not a weight) is a distinct coupling position absent from E6. 4: names the assignment collapse, the effect and a diagnostic. 4: eta_i = 0 is an exact identity control, the Euclidean target the geometry control, with directions stated. 4: one extra exp/log per item, stop-gradient table per epoch, eta bounded in [0,1/2]. 3: substantially different lever with a clean lesson either way.

SCORE: KAPPA_BRANCH | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=4 | implementability=4 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: KAPPA_BRANCH | IN_SCOPE | Per-item branch entropy H_i (training-only, deduplicated per user, add-one smoothed) sets kappa_i; the harmonic-mean pair curvature enters both the nearest-code rule and the commitment term, so codebook gradients and the SID move with the graph; kappa_i const is behaviour-off and the flat metric is geometry-off. No free constant (kappa0 = 1, beta = 0.25 baseline default).
EVIDENCE: KAPPA_BRANCH | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E8 E10 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: KAPPA_BRANCH | The field is nearly constant on this data: h_i = H_i/log(|S_i|+1) on the measured E5 bins gives 0.931/1.335 = 0.70, 1.505/1.723 = 0.87, 1.875/2.041 = 0.92, 2.362/2.485 = 0.95, 3.405/3.813 = 0.89, so kappa_i spans only about [1.70,1.95] on a kappa0 = 1 scale; the single run tests a nearly global curvature about 1.8*k0, and the null is then uninformative about the hypothesis (the proposal's own risk 2, quantified).
COUNTEREXAMPLE: KAPPA_BRANCH | Normalising by the item's own support collapses the measured contrast: items with top-1 mass 0.4375 and 0.0543 (a factor 47 in E5) differ by under 5% in kappa, so "broad-appeal hubs get more separation" is numerically void under the chosen normalisation.
RATIONALE: KAPPA_BRANCH | 4: same measured base (E5 with coverage) plus per-user dedup and smoothing against power-user domination; not 5 because the entropy-to-capacity hypothesis is unmeasured. 3: the curved distance changes shape, not just scale, and kbar <= max(kappa_i,kappa_j) keeps the ball argument positive (no domain error), but no matched Euclidean control is argued. 3: distinct from E6's arms. 4: occupancy gap, first-token entropy, recall, all with direction. 4: three controls plus a discriminating pair (occupancy gap and recall must both move). 4: differentiable, arcosh argument >= 1 by construction, under 5% cost; residual risk is oscillation from the non-metric pair curvature. 3: different coupling point with an informative negative.

SCORE: GEO_REPAIR | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=4 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: GEO_REPAIR | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour (training in-degree for the representative, successor Frechet mean for the move) and curvature (Poincare distance from that mean to candidate level-3 codes) meet in one decision rule rewriting the emitted SID for the colliding tail; removing either changes which code each duplicate receives. Weakness: "free capacity" is undefined in a capacity-free RQ-VAE and the geometry is a post-hoc ranking with no gradient.
EVIDENCE: GEO_REPAIR | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E8 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: GEO_REPAIR | The repair runs after the optimiser on the assignment only, so encoder, decoder and codebooks never see the repaired SIDs: item i's content point is still nearest its old code while Stage 3 is trained on the repaired labels, and with no gradient the content prior fights the assignment instead of reinforcing it.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GEO_REPAIR | Two items in the same collision class with S_i = S_i' share the Frechet mean m_i = m_i', so argmin over K_i returns the same code for both and the pair stays collided; the "rise in distinct SIDs" observable is not implied by the rule.
RATIONALE: GEO_REPAIR | 3: the collision cap is plausible and testable but the bundle has no collision count; E7 (l1_br T1/T2/T3/T4 = 0/0/0/9.03) hints at deeper-level degeneracy, suggestive but not a measured statistic - this is the UNKNOWN, resolved by the frozen-baseline SID group-size histogram. 2: the Poincare ranking of codes from a mean is a static rule a Euclidean nearest-code rule reproduces on nearly all items, and E15's control is not argued. 3: post-hoc geodesic collision repair is not in the ledger and the coupling position (decision rule) is distinct. 4: names the level-3 assignment change, distinct-SID rise, in-degree share and recall with directions. 4: three controls and the requirement that assignment shift and recall gain co-occur. 3: O(N log N) grouping plus a batched kernel is cheap, but "free capacity" and per-epoch discontinuity are unguarded. 3: a different failure mode (reachability) whose negative is informative.

SCORE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=2 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour (PMI-adjusted directed transition mass aggregated into per-level M_l) and curvature (hyperboloid, trainable rho, geodesic log/exp at the parent centroid) act on the same centroids in the same quantiser, so removing M_l or flattening the space changes the SID. The three switch names are not enumerated and the 0.1 cone weight is a chosen constant, contrary to the no-tuning policy.
EVIDENCE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | The cone hinge is algebraically vacuous and inverted. On one hyperboloid sheet <y-x,y-x>_L = <y,y>_L - 2<x,y>_L + <x,x>_L = (2/c^2)(cosh(c d) - 1) >= 0 for every pair, so the "future cone" condition <= 0 holds only at d = 0 and the violation indicator is 1 for all distinct centroids; the hinge is therefore always active and, increasing in cosh(c d), it MINIMISES the geodesic distance between transition-mass pairs - a transition attraction, the opposite of a causal order - and the advertised diagnostic ("cone violation rate falls") cannot move.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | With c = 1 and two centroids at d_L = 1: <Delta,Delta>_L = 2(cosh 1 - 1) = 1.086 > 0, so the pair is "violating" regardless of geometry, and d(1.086 + m)/dd = 2 sinh(1) > 0 pulls the pair together instead of ordering it.
RATIONALE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | 3: PMI-adjustment is the right popularity correction and E5 shows direction is informative for low-entropy items, but aggregation onto level-1 code pairs is unmeasured and self-declared sparse. 2: the surviving curved computation reduces to a mass-weighted geodesic attraction a Euclidean transition-attraction term reproduces after a monotone transform; trainable rho is not shown necessary. 3: Lorentz causal-cone ordering is not in E6's ledger and the coupling position (ordering the centroids that also serve as log/exp bases) is distinct. 2: the stated chain is broken - the observable is identically zero and the mechanism does the reverse, so the expected direction is not the one claimed. 3: the L_rec/L_rq/L_cone decomposition allows a droppable behaviour term and a flattenable geometry, but the arms are unnamed and 0.1 is not derived. 3: log/exp and arcosh are specified with a rho clamp, but the weight and [-1.5,1.5] clamp are chosen and the sign error means the code implements something else. 3: a genuinely different geometric device whose negative would be informative about cone/order structure.